00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:09.909
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:13.305
I would say there's two clear signs that
would indicate when a conflict is

00:00:13.305 --> 00:00:16.990
about to become a negative conflict for
the team or for the business.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:20.690
The first sign that a conflict is going to
become a negative force in your team,

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.520
is if the emotions are starting to
escalate.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.110
If people are getting angry or if they're
getting upset.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.050
This is a sign that the conflict is going
to become personal,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.210
is going to become distracting from task
performance.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.090
The second sign that a conflict is going
to be destructive for a team, is that

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:39.450
people are fighting about things that they
actually don't really care about or

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.160
shouldn't be that important.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.940
So, if your fighting about something very
silly about what time to meet or

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.580
who's gonna do what.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.270
Often these types of conflicts are venues
for people to fight about bigger issues.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.710
Be it status concerns, leadership issues,
questions of respect.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.360
If you're fighting about something that
you just doesn't make sense,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.850
why this is such a big issue, that often
means that your team is in trouble and

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.200
this is a destructive conflict.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.522
And they need to take a step back to
figure out, what the real issues are and

00:01:05.522 --> 00:01:06.439
go from there.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:08.619
[MUSIC]

00:01:08.619 --> 00:01:12.516
Conflicts generally start with one person
who's listening to a group discussion and

00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:15.870
thinks gee, I really don't agree with what
is being said here.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.460
I should say something.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.250
So, the person expresses conflict, and the
other people on the team have

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.750
the choice to reflect on that and to offer
feedback to agree or disagree.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.090
Generally, one person will offer feedback
to what the person says, so you think we

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:33.120
should take strategy x, well actually I
think we should take strategy y.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.818
At this point you have an interpersonal
conflict between two people on the team.

00:01:36.818 --> 00:01:38.820
The other five team members could be
sitting there watching and

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.090
deciding whether or not they should choose
to engage or not.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.190
Conflicts at this stage generally are the
most positive for

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.870
team performance, because the two people
engaging,

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:49.560
are engaging because of authentic interest
in the issue, and

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.010
differences in opinion are information and
they need to be reconciled.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.880
However, as the conflict starts to
escalate and

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.700
to grow and to involve more members,
members may not always join.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.410
For reasons due to the, the question being
fought about,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.130
they could join because they get
emotional,

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.490
if people are starting to yell or get
angry and they also get emotional.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.790
Could also be that they're helping
political alliances their boss, or

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:13.700
their friend or someone they're close to
is involved in the conflict, and

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.020
they feel they may need to join to support
them.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.250
This is a second phase of conflict and
where subgroups start to form.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.330
And when the subgroup's involved in a
conflict, again it intensifies, it

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.600
escalates, it's more likely to become more
personal and destructive than the initial

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.530
conflict between the two people, who had
just different pieces of information.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.610
And as these subgroups start to fight with
one another over time,

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.490
any remaining members that weren't
involved in the conflict have no choice.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.879
They're going to be sucked in as well and
then you have a true team brawl,

00:02:39.879 --> 00:02:42.620
which is not going to be a good thing for
team performance.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:46.799
[MUSIC]

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:50.925
If a manager detects a conflict in the
team, the first thing the manager should

00:02:50.925 --> 00:02:55.150
do is to identify what's being discussed
and who's involved and why.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.960
So, if he sees two members, or she sees
two members discussing an issue that's

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.900
very related to the task and the members
seem to be engaging in this debate.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.010
Because they want to make the team better.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.650
As a manager, this is a conflict that you
